  "slug": "visual-activity-hierarchical-2c1b",
  "summary": "Recent preprints and a published method pair biologically realistic inputs and memory structure with quantitative fits to neural and behavioral data.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is explicit computational models evaluated quantitatively against neural or behavioral data. A sensory-realistic network matches human working-memory behavior more closely than abstract-input models, and the gain is attributed mainly to input realism. NeuroHorizon forecasts macaque and mouse neural population activity up to 1 s ahead using hierarchical prediction memory and scheduled sampling. A probabilistic normative model of visual segmentation reproduces single-neuron gain and variability through recurrent feedback. FDTest applies a generalized fluctuation-dissipation test to quantify local memory effects in whole-brain models. A tutorial on neural simulation-based inference addresses cognitive models with intractable likelihoods. What is new is the emphasis on biologically grounded inputs, memory structure, and feedback, with claims tied to quantitative comparison. The scope across these works is heterogeneous, so this thread is dominant rather than shared by every item.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-17"
}
